WEBVTT

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:07.177
Every refinery plan ends with one word, printed by a program nobody in the plant can read.

00:00:07.177 --> 00:00:07.576
Optimal.

00:00:08.876 --> 00:00:18.740
Crude purchases, unit runs and blends are decided this way every week, on licensed software that no one here can inspect, audit or extend.

00:00:19.140 --> 00:00:20.849
A planner needs three answers.

00:00:20.849 --> 00:00:22.558
Is this the best plan.

00:00:22.558 --> 00:00:24.266
Does it keep every limit.

00:00:24.266 --> 00:00:27.684
And if no plan exists, which limits are to blame.

00:00:28.084 --> 00:00:30.442
And a wrong answer does not crash.

00:00:30.442 --> 00:00:37.180
A plan that breaks a quality limit by one part in a thousand runs, prints, and ships a bad blend.

00:00:37.580 --> 00:00:42.124
That is why a refinery asked for a solver of its own.

00:00:42.124 --> 00:00:50.455
The problem statement is plain: an indigenous optimization solver, a sovereign alternative to Xpress and CPLEX, issued by Mangalore Refinery and Petrochemicals.

00:00:50.455 --> 00:00:53.105
Not a wrapper around someone else's engine.

00:00:53.105 --> 00:01:04.844
Something built here, with its source open, that the people who depend on it can read, audit and extend, and with nothing borrowed from the solvers it is meant to replace.

00:01:05.244 --> 00:01:06.293
This is SANKHYA.

00:01:06.293 --> 00:01:12.235
An optimization solver written from the mathematics, that does not ask you to trust the word optimal.

00:01:12.235 --> 00:01:13.284
It proves it.

00:01:14.584 --> 00:01:17.308
A model goes in through one entry point.

00:01:17.308 --> 00:01:23.437
It is presolved, handed by its class to one of seven engines, and returned to the original model.

00:01:23.437 --> 00:01:25.480
Then a separate program checks it.

00:01:25.880 --> 00:01:31.020
That separate program is the verifier, and it shares no code with the solver.

00:01:31.020 --> 00:01:35.426
It reads only two things: the model file and the answer file.

00:01:35.426 --> 00:01:38.731
And it will not accept a number on trust.

00:01:38.731 --> 00:01:42.770
For an optimum, it wants a dual that matches the primal.

00:01:42.770 --> 00:01:49.379
For an impossible model, it wants a certificate: weights on the constraints that, added up, contradict each other.

00:01:49.379 --> 00:01:54.152
For an unbounded one, a direction along which the cost never stops improving.

00:01:54.552 --> 00:01:56.825
Here is a small crude blend.

00:01:56.825 --> 00:01:59.856
The dual simplex solves it in two iterations.

00:01:59.856 --> 00:02:05.160
The answer file carries every row's price, and how far each price stays valid.

00:02:05.560 --> 00:02:09.937
Then the verifier, in its own arithmetic.

00:02:09.937 --> 00:02:14.940
Bounds, rows, reduced costs, complementary slackness, strong duality.

00:02:14.940 --> 00:02:16.191
Fourteen checks.

00:02:16.191 --> 00:02:16.816
Verified.

00:02:17.216 --> 00:02:20.090
Now tighten the limits until no plan exists.

00:02:20.090 --> 00:02:22.605
The solver does not just say infeasible.

00:02:22.605 --> 00:02:27.276
It hands over the proof, and the four constraints that cannot all hold.

00:02:27.276 --> 00:02:29.072
The verifier checks that too.

00:02:29.472 --> 00:02:34.727
Pooling, the hard part of blending, has products of variables and false optima.

00:02:34.727 --> 00:02:42.408
With the global option, spatial branch and bound reaches Haverly's published optimum of four hundred, and proves the bound.

00:02:42.808 --> 00:02:47.523
Every result is a committed file with the commit and the machine.

00:02:47.523 --> 00:02:53.416
On Netlib, eighty-one of eighty-nine match the published optimum, and all eighty-nine pass the verifier.

00:02:53.816 --> 00:02:56.907
And the losses are published beside the wins.

00:02:56.907 --> 00:02:59.226
Eleven of thirty mixed-integer models proved.

00:02:59.226 --> 00:03:01.930
Two of eight of the largest LPs.

00:03:01.930 --> 00:03:05.408
Every one named, with the file that measured it.

00:03:05.808 --> 00:03:12.969
Against HiGHS, run as a separate process on the same machine, the answers agree on all fifty medium Netlib models.

00:03:12.969 --> 00:03:20.130
The time depends on the machine: level with it on a rented server, about twice its time on a laptop.

00:03:20.130 --> 00:03:23.352
Both readings are kept, and neither is quoted alone.

00:03:23.752 --> 00:03:26.926
None of this is a promise in a README.

00:03:26.926 --> 00:03:30.804
CI fails the build if a solver library is ever linked.

00:03:30.804 --> 00:03:33.978
A fuzz test holds the simplex against exact arithmetic.

00:03:33.978 --> 00:03:37.504
And an optimal the numbers do not support is downgraded.

00:03:37.904 --> 00:03:39.980
And some of it is new.

00:03:39.980 --> 00:03:45.518
An engine race, where three engines run at once and only a verified answer may win.

00:03:45.518 --> 00:03:48.979
A proof of a mixed-integer optimum, checked in exact fractions.

00:03:48.979 --> 00:03:51.747
Bounds that stay valid whatever the rounding did.

00:03:51.747 --> 00:03:55.208
Each is off by default until a measurement earns it.

00:03:55.608 --> 00:03:57.542
Here is the whole picture.

00:03:57.542 --> 00:03:59.477
Seven engines behind one call.

00:03:59.477 --> 00:04:05.666
Dual and primal simplex, which produce the basis that warm starts and branch and bound need.

00:04:05.666 --> 00:04:08.374
An interior point for large sparse models.

00:04:08.374 --> 00:04:11.856
A first-order method that also runs on NVIDIA GPUs.

00:04:11.856 --> 00:04:14.177
Two engines for convex quadratic programs.

00:04:14.177 --> 00:04:19.206
Branch and cut for integer models, and spatial branch and bound for pooling.

00:04:19.206 --> 00:04:28.104
Every one written from the papers, in C plus plus, and every one returns the same kind of answer to the same verifier.

00:04:28.504 --> 00:04:30.720
It is real and it is public.

00:04:30.720 --> 00:04:36.736
This is the build, this is the test run, and these are the seven engines the solver itself reports.

00:04:37.136 --> 00:04:39.963
The GPU is a measurement, not a slogan.

00:04:39.963 --> 00:04:45.972
The first-order engine has a CUDA port of its own, and it was measured on three cards.

00:04:45.972 --> 00:04:52.687
On a datacenter L4, at ten thousand rows, it is four point two one times faster than the processor.

00:04:52.687 --> 00:05:00.109
Below the crossover, on smaller models, the GPU is slower, because launching work on the card costs more than it saves.

00:05:00.109 --> 00:05:02.936
The site says both, and names the files.

00:05:03.336 --> 00:05:07.557
And you do not have to take any of this from us.

00:05:07.557 --> 00:05:17.758
On Windows, Ubuntu or macOS, five commands take a fresh clone to a verified answer: configure, build, run the tests, solve a model, and check it with the verifier.

00:05:17.758 --> 00:05:26.904
The benchmark instances are committed, so nothing is downloaded after the clone, and every number in this film can be produced again on your own machine.

00:05:27.304 --> 00:05:28.242
What we learned.

00:05:28.242 --> 00:05:31.058
A wrong answer looks exactly like a right one.

00:05:31.058 --> 00:05:32.935
A proof is cheaper than trust.

00:05:32.935 --> 00:05:36.376
And a published loss is worth more than a hidden win.

00:05:36.776 --> 00:05:37.300
SANKHYA.

00:05:37.300 --> 00:05:38.872
Don't trust optimal.

00:05:38.872 --> 00:05:39.920
Check it.
